<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That Is a Circle.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize simple classroom instructions spoken slowly and clearly. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple classroom instructions about shapes using gestures or actions (e.g., "Point to the square." "Find the triangle."). Can point to and name shapes during activities when prompted (e.g., "What is this?" Student points to a circle and says, "circle"). Can respond to very simple shape-related commands by pointing.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a simple "Hello" song. Then, the teacher says, "Look around! What do you see?" and points to various classroom objects like a clock, a book, a whiteboard. The teacher asks, "What shape is this?" pointing to a round clock, modeling "It is a circle. The clock is a circle." The teacher shows large, colorful flashcards of basic shapes: circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval, star, diamond. Students repeat the shape names after the teacher chorally.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, for example, a circle, and says clearly, "This is a circle. It is a circle." The teacher then holds up a square flashcard and says, "This is a square." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /s/ in "square" and /r/ in "rectangle," exaggerating the sounds. The teacher uses gestures, tracing the shapes in the air, and points to real objects in the classroom that match the shapes (e.g., a square table, a rectangle door).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Holding up a circle flashcard and asking, "Is this a square?" (Students shake heads or say "No" with gestures, pointing to the correct shape if available).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Holding up a square flashcard and asking, "What is this?" (Students point to the flashcard or say "Square" or make a squaring gesture).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Saying, "Point to the circle!" and observing if students point to the correct flashcard or a round object in the room.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short, repetitive shape recognition chant/song (audio or teacher-led) using flashcards of circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval. Example chant: "Look, look, look! What do you see? I see a circle, big for me! (<i>Students point to a big circle.</i>) I see a square, a little one! (<i>Students point to a small square.</i>) Let's find some shapes, this is fun!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes: Recognize and name shapes like squares and circles during classroom activities.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays/sings the chant twice. Students listen carefully.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are we listening about? (Shapes!)" (Teacher prompts with gestures).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays the chant again, pausing after each shape mention. Students listen and point to the corresponding shape flashcard that the teacher is holding or that is displayed on the board. For "big" or "small," students use their hands to show the size. For "round," students make a circular gesture.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Clap:</b> Students listen to the chant and clap once every time they hear the word "circle" or "square."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students listen and respond to simple instructions about shapes. Vocabulary: square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, star, big, small, round, find, point, look. Grammar: "That is a circle." "What shape is it?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "Teacher Says, Point!" (10 min):</b> The teacher lays out flashcards of all target shapes (circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval, diamond, star) on a table or on the floor. The teacher says simple commands like: "Point to the circle!" "Point to the square!" "Point to the triangle!" Students listen and point to the correct flashcard. The teacher repeats, speeding up slightly or varying the order to ensure attentive listening. Teacher also uses adjectives: "Point to the big square!" "Point to the small circle!"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - "Find the Shape!" (8 min):</b> The teacher gives commands, and students perform actions around the classroom. Examples: "Find something round!" (Students touch or point to a round object like a clock or ball). "Point to a square table!" (integrating "shape + noun"). "Look for something that is a rectangle!" "Draw a circle in the air!" "Draw a tall rectangle!" The teacher observes students' responses and provides encouragement or gentle correction if needed, repeating instructions slowly.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Listen and Show" (5 min):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small shape cutouts (circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval). Student A says (or gestures if unable to speak), "Show me a circle," and Student B listens and holds up the correct shape cutout. Then they switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and monitoring listening comprehension and basic shape recognition.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 23 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes Around Us".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Shape Detective Game" where students listen to descriptions and identify shapes in the classroom. This is a whole-class activity led by the teacher.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains: "Let's be shape detectives! Listen carefully to my words. Point to the shape I am talking about!"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher starts describing objects in the classroom using target vocabulary:
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I see something. It is a circle. It is big. Where is the big circle?" (Students point to a clock or a picture of the sun.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! It is a square. It is small. Where is a small square?" (Students point to a small book or a tile.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a rectangle. It is tall. Point to the tall rectangle!" (Students point to a door or a tall window.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find something round!" (Students point to anything round).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What shape is the sun? It is yellow. It is a circle!" (Teacher models, students point to a sun picture/drawing).</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher repeats descriptions and instructions slowly and clearly, using gestures to aid comprehension. Students respond by pointing to the correct shape or object. The focus is on their listening comprehension and non-verbal response.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Shape Detective Game" and "Listen and Color" activity.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Color/Circle" worksheet. Students receive a worksheet with various shapes (circle, square, triangle, rectangle). The teacher gives instructions:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Color the big circle yellow!"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Circle the small square!"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Color the rectangle blue!"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a star next to the triangle!"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Student correctly identifies and points to shapes when named by the teacher.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Student follows simple multi-step commands (e.g., "Color the big circle yellow").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Shape Recognition:</b> Student points to and/or performs actions related to target shapes (circle, square, rectangle, triangle, oval, diamond, star).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students orally complete sentences or point to emojis.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can point to a ____." (e.g., circle, square)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Finding shapes was: 😊 (happy face) / 😐 (neutral face) / 🙁 (sad face)." (Students point to the appropriate emoji drawn on the board.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new shape did you hear today that you liked?" (Students can point to a flashcard or say the name if able).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun to find in the classroom?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR commands, the chant, student engagement with pointing tasks).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., more visual aids for specific shapes like diamond/oval, slower pace for certain instructions, more opportunities for small group practice).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students struggled with specific shape names or instructions? How can I support them?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a simple text (e.g., a short illustrated book or worksheet) that uses the same shape vocabulary. They will listen to the teacher read and follow along, practicing identifying the shapes from the text, building on their receptive skills.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 4 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>